# 2. Detailed Pattern Analysis

## Pattern: Feature-first / modular architecture
- Confidence: Clearly used
- Category: Architectural
- Evidence: The code is organized by feature under lib/features, with separate auth, dashboard, homescreen, and variantscreen modules.
- Why this is this pattern: Each feature is isolated in its own folder rather than being grouped only by technical layer.
- Role in the project: Keeps the dashboard engine, runtime variant engine, auth, and home screen separated into independent modules.
- Benefits: Easier navigation, clearer ownership boundaries, and less cross-feature coupling.
- Trade-offs: Features can drift in structure quality; some are fully layered while others are thin or stubbed.

## Pattern: Layered architecture, Clean Architecture-inspired
- Confidence: Likely used
- Category: Architectural
- Evidence: Presentation and data layers are split in files like dashboard_cubit.dart, dashboard_repo.dart, and variant_repository.dart.
- Why this is this pattern: The UI depends on cubits, cubits depend on repositories, and repositories depend on data sources or loaders.
- Role in the project: Separates UI concerns from data retrieval and runtime config loading.
- Benefits: Better testability, smaller change surface, and easier replacement of data sources.
- Trade-offs: This is not full textbook Clean Architecture because there is no explicit use-case/interactor layer and some flows go directly from cubit to repository or loader.

## Pattern: Repository pattern
- Confidence: Clearly used
- Category: Data access
- Evidence: dashboard_repo.dart with dashboard_repo_impl.dart, variant_repository.dart with variant_repository.dart, and auth_repo.dart with auth_repo_impl.dart.
- Why this is this pattern: Each feature exposes an abstraction and a concrete implementation that hides the actual source of data.
- Role in the project: Decouples cubits and screens from asset loading, mock providers, and API wrapper details.
- Benefits: Easier swapping of data sources, better test seams, and cleaner dependency direction.
- Trade-offs: Some repositories are very thin pass-through layers, so the abstraction can feel heavier than the logic it currently contains.

## Pattern: Data source pattern
- Confidence: Clearly used
- Category: Data access
- Evidence: dashboard_data_provider.dart acts as an in-memory source, while variant_repository.dart loads JSON from assets.
- Why this is this pattern: The repository delegates to a data provider or asset-backed source instead of embedding the raw data directly in the UI.
- Role in the project: Supplies dashboard content and runtime variant configurations.
- Benefits: Makes it easy to swap mocked, asset-based, or remote data later.
- Trade-offs: The project currently mixes mock, asset, and potential remote styles, so source boundaries are not fully standardized.

## Pattern: DTO / mapper style config models
- Confidence: Clearly used
- Category: Data access
- Evidence: Immutable configuration objects such as app_config.dart, screen_config.dart, component_config.dart, and app_theme_model.dart, plus JSON parsing in config_loader.dart and variant_repository.dart.
- Why this is this pattern: JSON is translated into typed models before being rendered or consumed by the UI.
- Role in the project: Bridges JSON config files and Flutter widgets.
- Benefits: Clear structure, safer access to properties, and easier rendering logic.
- Trade-offs: Parsing and validation are spread across loaders and property parsers rather than centralized in dedicated mapper classes.

## Pattern: Dependency injection
- Confidence: Clearly used
- Category: Dependency management
- Evidence: Dependencies are injected via constructors in dashboard_cubit.dart and variant_screen.dart, then assembled in service_locator.dart.
- Why this is this pattern: Consumers do not instantiate their own collaborators directly.
- Role in the project: Keeps the app bootstrapping and route construction centralized.
- Benefits: Improves testability and makes the dependency graph easier to replace in one place.
- Trade-offs: The wiring is partly hidden behind GetIt, which makes call sites less explicit.

## Pattern: Service locator
- Confidence: Clearly used
- Category: Dependency management
- Evidence: A global service_locator.dart instance registers and resolves dependencies in main.dart, app_router.dart, and other shared code.
- Why this is this pattern: Objects are fetched from a central container rather than passed through every call site.
- Role in the project: Acts as the app-wide composition root.
- Benefits: Fast to wire up, convenient in bootstrap code, and common in Flutter apps with multiple shared services.
- Trade-offs: Hides dependencies, can make testing less transparent, and can grow into implicit global state if overused.

## Pattern: Cubit-based state management
- Confidence: Clearly used
- Category: State management
- Evidence: token_cubit.dart, shared_preferences_cubit.dart, dashboard_cubit.dart, and variant_cubit.dart, plus variant_screen.dart and variant_screen.dart.
- Why this is this pattern: The app uses Cubit state containers with explicit loading/success/failure transitions.
- Role in the project: Handles token loading, dashboard loading, and runtime variant loading.
- Benefits: Simple async state flow, easy to follow, and good fit for Flutter UIs.
- Trade-offs: State classes are defined per feature and not standardized through a shared base in practice, even though base_cubit.dart exists.

## Pattern: Observer pattern
- Confidence: Clearly used
- Category: Behavioral
- Evidence: app_bloc_observer.dart overrides lifecycle callbacks and is assigned in main.dart.
- Why this is this pattern: The app observes Cubit and Bloc lifecycle changes centrally without modifying each Cubit.
- Role in the project: Provides logging for creation, state transitions, errors, and closure.
- Benefits: Useful for debugging state flow and catching lifecycle issues.
- Trade-offs: Print-based logging is noisy and not ideal for production observability without a proper logging backend.

## Pattern: Composite pattern
- Confidence: Clearly used
- Category: Structural
- Evidence: component_config.dart can contain a child or children, and screen_renderer.dart recursively renders the tree.
- Why this is this pattern: The UI config is modeled as a tree of nodes that can be rendered uniformly, with parent nodes delegating to nested nodes.
- Role in the project: Powers the JSON-driven UI engine for the variant screens.
- Benefits: Naturally supports nested layout structures and makes the renderer extensible by node type.
- Trade-offs: Requires strict schema alignment between JSON, parser, and renderer implementations.

## Pattern: Strategy pattern
- Confidence: Likely used
- Category: Behavioral / Structural
- Evidence: component_renderer.dart defines interchangeable renderers, and ScreenRenderer.withPrimitives selects implementations from a map.
- Why this is this pattern: Each component type is rendered by a distinct strategy object chosen at runtime.
- Role in the project: Lets the tree engine support scaffold, column, row, container, text, button, and card without hardcoding each case into one huge renderer.
- Benefits: Keeps rendering logic separated by component type and simplifies adding new primitives.
- Trade-offs: The registry must stay synchronized with the enum and JSON schema, or unsupported components fail silently or render nothing.

## Pattern: Widget builder composition
- Confidence: Clearly used
- Category: UI composition
- Evidence: dashboard_screen.dart uses nested FutureBuilder and BlocBuilder, variant_screen.dart uses BlocProvider plus BlocBuilder, main.dart uses a Builder inside the app root, and home_screen.dart composes reusable private widgets like _VariantCard.
- Why this is this pattern: UI is composed from small widgets and builder widgets instead of a monolithic screen class.
- Role in the project: Handles async loading, conditional rendering, and reusable card-style UI.
- Benefits: Makes screens easier to reason about and keeps state-driven UI localized.
- Trade-offs: Nested builders can get deep and visually noisy if the flow grows.

# 3. Patterns Commonly Expected But NOT Clearly Present

- MVVM is not clearly present. There is no dedicated viewmodel layer separate from Cubit, and Cubits are doing the state orchestration directly.
- MVP is not clearly present. There is no explicit presenter layer mediating between view and model.
- MVC is not clearly present. The project does not organize code around controller/model/view triads.
- Hexagonal / Ports and Adapters is not clearly present in a strict sense. Repositories and loaders act like boundaries, but there is no explicit ports/use-case ring or adapter layering around the core.
- Full textbook Clean Architecture is not clearly present. The project is layered, but there is no explicit use-case/interactor layer and some flows are direct.
- GoF State pattern is not clearly present. The app uses state objects for Cubit state management, but not polymorphic state objects that change object behavior.
- Abstract Factory is not clearly present. ScreenRenderer.withPrimitives is a simple factory-style constructor, not a full abstract factory setup.
- Singleton is not clearly present as an application design pattern. GetIt provides a singleton container, but that is the service locator mechanism, not a meaningful domain singleton.
- Coordinator / navigator abstraction is not clearly present as a dedicated layer. Routing is centralized in app_router.dart, but screens still navigate directly with context.push.
- Command is not clearly present. There is no command queue or encapsulated action object model.

# 4. Final Architecture Characterization

This project primarily follows a feature-first layered architecture with repository-based data access, GetIt-backed dependency injection and service location, Cubit-based state management, and a JSON-driven runtime UI engine built around recursive composite rendering. The architecture is pragmatic rather than textbook: it is cleanly modular, but the full Clean Architecture stack is only partially implemented.

